{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radical ideal radical ideal","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md","fileName":"Radical Ideal.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radical Ideal","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:40\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","Ideal","Radical of an Ideal","Prime Ideal","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radical ideal radical ideal","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md","fileName":"Radical Ideal.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radical Ideal","heading":"Radical Ideal","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A radical ideal is an [[Ideal]] I satisfying I = √I. If a power rⁿ belongs to a radical ideal, then r already belongs to it.\n\nBecause the [[Radical of an Ideal]] is the intersection of all [[Prime Ideal]]s containing that ideal, radical ideals record which prime components occur while ignoring their multiplicities. Vanishing ideals of affine algebraic sets are radical in the classical setting developed by the source.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","Ideal","Radical of an Ideal","Prime Ideal","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radical ideal radical ideal","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radical Ideal.md","fileName":"Radical Ideal.md","folder":"